  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nSurface analysis shows the cooler Canadian air has pushed\nthrough all but the last southeastern counties of the CWA.\nBehind the front, temps are mostly in the 60s and 70s with brisk\nnortherly winds. Ahead of it, Falls City, Nebraska City, and\nsouthwest Iowa have managed 80 before the cooler air has arrived.\nKOAX has been interpreting returns from rain since before \nsunrise. Radar estimates suggest some locations between Norfolk \nand Sioux City (including Wayne) have received 1.5\". Ground \ntruth shows gauges mostly reporting closer to half of that. \n\nThese showers and associated lightning/thunder are slowly\nsliding east/southeast and are expected to dissipate and push \nout of the southeastern corner CWA by about 7pm tonight as the\nshortwave responsible for their forcing continues east.\n\nThe dry spell won't last long as another vort max - currently\nover the high country of Colorado - pushes through overnight.\nThat combined with the extra lift provided by the summer\nnocturnal jet, and likely PoPs (80-90%) are expected in the \nareas within 50 miles of I-80. With PWAT values near the 90th\npercentile for August, some localized heavy rain is possible in\nthe urban areas of Omaha and Lincoln. CAM QPF suggests rainfall\nof 3.5\" is possible at Omaha, which would overwhelm the city's\ndrainage system. Considered a flood watch, but will hold off for\nnow due to low confidence as to where the QPF bullseye is most\nlikely to set up, but western Iowa/Omaha metro has best odds. \n\nThe SPC added an area of \"marginal threat\" for severe weather\nthis evening. Most of the lift is displaced north of the front,\nbut just ahead of it, there is 1500 J/kg of CAPE and 40-50 knots\nof bulk shear. Should a storm tap into that instability, it\ncould produce some borderline severe weather. \n\nOvernight's instability will be elevated... leaving lots of\nthunder and lightning, but little severe threat.",
